    affiliation: "Universidade Estadual de Ponta Grossa"   
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5589-9015"  

repository-code: "https://github.com/icaromeidem/meidem"
url: "https://pypi.org/project/meidem/"
license: MIT

abstract: >
  When modeling exoplanet transits, accurately accounting for stellar limb darkening (LD) is essential for the precise determination 
  of planetary radii and orbital parameters. LD coefficients are typically derived by interpolating tabulated grids based on stellar 
  atmospheric models. However, managing heterogeneous table formats, distinct interpolation strategies, and manual data downloads across 
  different publications can be cumbersome and error-prone.
  
  MEIDEM (Multi-grid Epic Interpolator for stellar limb DarkEning Models) is an open-source Python package designed to solve this problem. 
  It provides a single, unified interface to interpolate LD coefficients from multiple published grids for any given set of stellar 
  parameters (effective temperature, surface gravity, metallicity, and microturbulence). All data tables are pre-bundled within the package,
  eliminating the need for manual downloads or path configurations.
  
  Currently, MEIDEM supports several standard reference grids in the exoplanet literature, including Kostogryz et al. (2022) and multiple 
  Claret grids (2011, 2017, 2022). It seamlessly handles various atmospheric models (ATLAS, PHOENIX, MPS-ATLAS), multiple LD laws 
  (e.g., power-2, quadratic, non-linear, 4-coefficient), and a wide array of photometric passbands (including TESS, Kepler, CHEOPS, 
  PLATO, Gaia, and SDSS). By abstracting the data-handling complexities, MEIDEM ensures reproducible, fast, and physically consistent 
  limb darkening calculations for exoplanet transit modeling and stellar astrophysics workflows.

keywords:
